   "id": 1,
   "title": "The fire warden role and the law",
   "minutes": 12,
   "html": "<p>A fire warden, sometimes called a fire marshal, is a person the organisation has chosen and trained to help keep people safe from fire. The role is not about fighting fires. It is about <strong>prevention, leadership and getting people out</strong>.</p>\n\n<h3>Where the role comes from</h3>\n<p>In England and Wales, workplace fire safety is governed by the <strong>Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005</strong>. The <strong>responsible person</strong>, usually the employer, must carry out a fire risk assessment, put fire precautions in place and plan for emergencies. Article 18 requires them to appoint one or more <strong>competent persons</strong> to help carry out the preventive and protective measures. They must make sure there are enough of these people, and that they have the training, time and equipment they need. Fire wardens are those competent persons. Scotland and Northern Ireland have their own laws that work in a very similar way.</p>\n<p>\"Competent\" means you have the training, experience and knowledge to do the job properly. That is why wardens are trained, and why that training is refreshed.</p>\n\n<h3>What a warden does</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Before a fire:</strong> keep an eye out for hazards, check escape routes and fire doors, report faults, help with drills, and know your area and the people in it.</li>\n<li><strong>During an alarm:</strong> help raise the alarm, lead people to safety, check your area, help those who need assistance and report to the person in charge.</li>\n<li><strong>After:</strong> help with the roll call, share what you know with the fire service, and take part in the review.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>What a warden is not</h3>\n<p>You are <strong>not expected to be a firefighter</strong>, and you must never put yourself at risk to save property. If a fire cannot be dealt with quickly and safely, your job is to get people out and call the fire service. Your own safety always comes first.</p>\n\n<h3>How many wardens?</h3>\n<p>There is no fixed number in law. The number depends on your fire risk assessment. A common rule of thumb is <strong>one warden per 50 people for low-risk premises, one per 20 for medium risk and one per 15 for high risk</strong>, with at least one for each floor or zone, and enough extra to cover holidays and sickness. Wardens need to be present whenever people are in the building, including shifts and weekends.</p>\n\n<h3>Your authority</h3>\n<p>During an evacuation, employees have a duty under Article 23 to cooperate with their employer on fire safety. In practice that means everyone follows instructions from wardens. Give clear, calm instructions, and make sure people know who the wardens are, for example by wearing a high-visibility vest or armband.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Key point:</strong> the law requires enough <em>competent</em> people, not a fixed number. Your role is to prevent, lead and help people out, not to fight fires.</div>",
