   "id": 2,
   "title": "Prevention and daily checks",
   "minutes": 15,
   "html": "<p>The best fire is the one that never starts, and the second best is the one that is found early. Wardens are the people who notice.</p>\n\n<h3>Where workplace fires start</h3>\n<p>Fires need fuel, heat and oxygen. In most workplaces the common ignition sources are <strong>electrical equipment and wiring</strong>, <strong>cooking</strong>, <strong>heaters</strong>, <strong>smoking materials</strong>, <strong>hot work</strong> and <strong>arson</strong>. The fuel is usually ordinary stuff: paper, packaging, furniture, waste and textiles. Prevention means keeping ignition sources and fuel apart.</p>\n\n<h3>Your regular walk-round</h3>\n<p>Do a quick check of your area at the start of each shift or day, and look at it again before you leave. Ask:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Escape routes:</strong> are corridors, stairs and exits clear? Nothing stored or left in them?</li>\n<li><strong>Fire doors:</strong> shut, undamaged, closing fully, and not wedged open?</li>\n<li><strong>Exit doors:</strong> can they be opened from inside without a key?</li>\n<li><strong>Signs and lights:</strong> are exit signs visible and emergency lights unobstructed?</li>\n<li><strong>Extinguishers:</strong> in place, with the safety pin and seal intact, and not blocked?</li>\n<li><strong>Electrics:</strong> any overloaded sockets, daisy-chained extension leads or damaged cables?</li>\n<li><strong>Housekeeping:</strong> waste and packaging away from heat and out of plant rooms, no build-up of rubbish.</li>\n<li><strong>Batteries:</strong> are lithium-ion batteries such as e-bikes and scooters charged unattended, overnight or in an escape route?</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>The routine tests</h3>\n<p>Your building's fire precautions have to be tested and maintained. A typical schedule is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Weekly:</strong> fire alarm test, using a different call point each week, at a known time.</li>\n<li><strong>Monthly:</strong> a quick visual check of each extinguisher, and a short function test of the emergency lighting.</li>\n<li><strong>Every year:</strong> a service of the extinguishers by a competent person, and a full-duration test of the emergency lighting, usually three hours.</li>\n<li><strong>Regularly:</strong> checks of fire doors and escape routes.</li>\n<li><strong>Every six months (or as the system requires):</strong> a service of the fire alarm by an engineer.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every check, fault and repair should go into the <strong>fire safety log book</strong>. A fault that is noticed but not recorded or reported is a fault that is still there on the day of the fire.</p>\n\n<h3>If you find a problem</h3>\n<p>Fix what you safely can, for example moving boxes out of a corridor. Report anything else straight away to the person responsible, and record it. If it is dangerous, such as a blocked final exit, don't leave it until later.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Key point:</strong> walk the route as if you'd never seen it. If you couldn't get out in the dark or in smoke, fix it or report it now.</div>",
