   "id": 3,
   "title": "Raising the alarm and taking charge",
   "minutes": 12,
   "html": "<p>The first minutes decide how a fire ends. Fires double in size quickly, and smoke can fill an escape route faster than people expect. Act early and clearly.</p>\n\n<h3>If you discover a fire</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Raise the alarm.</strong> Shout and operate the nearest manual call point.</li>\n<li><strong>Get people out</strong> of the immediate area and away from the fire.</li>\n<li><strong>Close the door</strong> of the room on your way out if it is safe to do so. A closed door slows smoke and flames.</li>\n<li><strong>Call 999</strong> (or 112) from a safe place once you are out, even if you think the alarm has already called them. Give the address, say it is a fire, and what is burning if you know.</li>\n<li><strong>Go to the assembly point</strong> and tell the person in charge what you saw.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Only consider tackling a small fire if you are trained, the alarm has been raised and you have a clear escape route (see Module 6).</p>\n\n<h3>If the alarm sounds</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Treat every alarm as <strong>real</strong> unless you have been told it is a planned test. Don't go and look to see if it's real.</li>\n<li>Put on your hi-vis, take your warden's list or tablet if you have one, and go to your zone.</li>\n<li>Tell people to leave <strong>now</strong>, without collecting belongings, and to use the nearest safe exit.</li>\n<li><strong>Don't use lifts</strong>, unless they are designated evacuation lifts and your procedure says to use them.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Giving clear instructions</h3>\n<p>People under stress follow short, calm instructions from someone who sounds in control. Say what to do, not what you fear. <em>\"Leave now, use the stairs on your left, go to the car park.\"</em> Avoid long explanations, and don't argue. If someone refuses to leave, make sure they understand it is a real risk, don't put yourself in danger, and report them to the person in charge.</p>\n\n<h3>The alarm panel and the fire service</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>fire alarm panel</strong> shows which zone has activated. A designated person should check it, and tell the fire service.</li>\n<li>Never <strong>silence or reset</strong> the alarm until the fire service say so.</li>\n<li>Someone should <strong>meet the fire service</strong> at the entrance. Tell them where the fire is, whether anyone is missing or may need help, and any special hazards such as gas, chemicals, cylinders or batteries.</li>\n<li>Have a plan of the building ready if you can, and know the location of isolation points for gas and electricity.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>When the alarm is a false alarm</h3>\n<p>Don't assume it. The fire service or the person in charge decides when it is safe to return. Record the cause afterwards, so that the cause can be fixed. Repeated false alarms are a risk because people start to ignore them.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Key point:</strong> every alarm is a real fire until the person in charge says otherwise. Leave first, investigate never.</div>",
